Step 4: Complexity Analysis
Time Complexity:
├─ With binary heap (priority queue): O((V + E) log V)
├─ V = intersections, E = roads
├─ Real city: V ≈ 1M, E ≈ 3M
├─ Calculation: (1M + 3M) × log(1M) ≈ 80M operations
├─ Modern CPU: ~1 billion ops/sec
├─ Result: < 0.1 seconds ✓
Space Complexity:
├─ Priority queue: O(V)
├─ Distance map: O(V)
├─ Previous map: O(V)
├─ Total: O(V) = ~1M nodes
├─ Memory: ~50 MB (very reasonable)
Google Maps optimizations:
├─ A* algorithm (heuristic-guided Dijkstra)
├─ Bidirectional search (meet in middle)
├─ Contraction hierarchies (precomputed shortcuts)
├─ Real result: 0.01 seconds for global routing!
Step 5: Why This Works
The Greedy Choice Property:
Why Dijkstra is guaranteed optimal:
Proof sketch:
1. We always pick the node with minimum distance
2. In positive-weight graphs, once visited, distance is final
3. Why? Any other path goes through unvisited nodes
4. Those nodes have ≥ current distance
5. So they can't improve the path!
Visual proof:
Start ─5─ A ─3─ Goal
│ ↗
└─ 10 ─ B ─2─┘
When we visit A (distance 5):
- Could path through B be better?
- B's distance ≥ 10 (longer than A's 5)
- B → Goal = 10 + 2 = 12
- A → Goal = 5 + 3 = 8 ✓ Better!
- Greedy choice was optimal!
